  {
   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Why the accent is there",
     "texto": [
      "Spanish spelling tells you the stress.\n\n1. A word ending in a **vowel, n or s** is stressed on the second-to-last syllable: **ca**sa, **ha**blan, **lu**nes.\n\n2. A word ending in **any other consonant** is stressed on the last syllable: ha**blar**, ciu**dad**, re**loj**.\n\n3. A **tilde** marks every word that breaks rules 1 and 2: ca**fé**, tam**bién**, in**glés**; **ár**bol, **fá**cil, **lá**piz.\n\n4. A word stressed on the third-to-last syllable **always** has a tilde: **mé**dico, te**lé**fono, **dí**melo.\n\nA stressed **i** or **u** next to a, e, o takes a tilde to stand on its own: pa**ís**, d**í**a, r**í**o.\n\nA tilde also separates pairs of short words (see the grammar section) and marks question words, even inside a sentence: no sé **dónde** vive.\n\nPlurals can gain or lose it: joven → **jó**venes; inglés → ingleses; examen → e**xá**menes."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Three kinds of word and tilde pairs",
     "texto": [
      "**Oxytone words** are stressed on the last syllable. They take a tilde if they end in a vowel, n or s: **café, también, inglés**. No tilde: **reloj, ciudad**.\n\n**Paroxytone words** are stressed on the second-to-last. They take a tilde if they do NOT end in a vowel, n or s: **árbol, fácil, lápiz**. No tilde: **casa, lunes**.\n\n**Proparoxytone words** are stressed on the third-to-last. They always take a tilde: **médico, teléfono, sábado**.\n\n**Pairs that the tilde separates**:\n\n- **él** (pronoun) and **el** (article)\n- **tú** (pronoun) and **tu** (possessive)\n- **mí** (pronoun) and **mi** (possessive)\n- **sí** (yes) and **si** (if)\n- **sé** (I know) and **se** (pronoun)\n- **té** (tea) and **te** (pronoun)\n- **más** (more) and **mas** (but, old-fashioned)\n- **qué, cuándo, dónde** (questions) and **que, cuando, donde** (connectors)"
     ],
